sections.s2_technical:
Because StringBuilder inherits Object's identity-based equals and hashCode, lookups by a freshly built StringBuilder with identical text return null even before any mutation.

```java
import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Map;

Map<StringBuilder, String> bad = new HashMap<>();
StringBuilder key = new StringBuilder("SAV2001");
bad.put(key, "Rajesh");
System.out.println(bad.get(new StringBuilder("SAV2001"))); // null — identity equals
key.append("-X");                                          // mutate after insert
System.out.println(bad.get(key));                          // null — bucket now wrong

Map<String, String> good = new HashMap<>();
good.put("SAV2001", "Rajesh");
System.out.println(good.get("SAV2001"));                   // Rajesh — immutable, correct
```

The senior point: good HashMap keys are immutable and have value-based equals and hashCode. String, Integer, and BigDecimal all qualify; StringBuilder does not.

